Overview
The s003 subgraph contained 22 non-ambiguous candidates. The organizational-culture/leadership candidate and culture-specific evidence within the sociocultural category were excluded. The remaining higher-order dependencies are ranked by summed evidence_count; these scores measure evidence density, not effect size. The bounded cases predominantly concern healthcare education, clinical decision support and medical knowledge preservation, so they should not be treated as representative of healthcare generally or of the construction sector. Digital codification dominates data, modelling and repository practices, while experiential amplification characterizes XR-supported clinical rehearsal.
Digitally reconfigured dependencies
1. Clinically reliable data, algorithm and validation infrastructure (66). Three-dimensional models omitted surgical margins; AI education required high-quality training data and risked bias; federated-learning performance varied across providers with limited datasets. Wearable blood-pressure models incurred retraining overhead, osteosarcoma knowledge graphs underrepresented low-frequency entities, and registry data were delayed, incomplete or incorrectly reported. Deployment therefore requires interoperable clinical data, domain annotation, physiological constraints, representative validation samples and continuous accuracy auditing. Without these capabilities, digital codification can institutionalize unsafe or non-generalizable knowledge (c209;c213;c215;c222;c227).
2. Adaptive clinical-learning and user-safety design (65). Static lectures, anatomical atlases and slide-based nuclear-safety training inadequately represented spatial or hazardous practice. AR, VR and mixed reality enabled situated rehearsal of anatomy, overdose response and disaster procedures, yet produced cognitive overload, dizziness, headset discomfort and interface difficulties. Knowledge also declined without retrieval and refresher practice. Deployment is conditional on staged instruction, individualization, accessible interfaces, feedback, repeated rehearsal and behavioural assessment; immersion alone does not demonstrate clinical transfer (c209;c210;c214;c216;c225).
3. Connected clinical-device, site and resource infrastructure (31). Wearables faced processing and storage constraints, mixed-reality training required physical space and multiple headsets, and online programmes depended on reliable internet and suitable devices. Legacy Android incompatibility restricted AR use, while digitization relied on access to medical literature and scarce manuscripts or anatomical resources. Deployment consequently requires connectivity, compatible devices, computing capacity, training space and dependable source-material supply; infrastructural inequality directly limits participation and scalability (c209;c215;c216;c218;c221;c225).
Persistently established dependencies
1. Protected clinical expertise, technical support and staff time (21). Producing anatomical models required specialist skills and time; nursing educators reported insufficient technical support; clinicians faced heavy workloads and course-time constraints. Registry users also lacked expertise and role-defined time for interpreting performance data. Although algorithms and online learning reduced some search and travel demands, deployment remained conditional on technical personnel, clinical experts and protected learning or validation time (c209;c217;c218;c224;c227).
2. Healthcare knowledge-continuity and information-governance architecture (14). Offline communication could not meet complex medical-information demand, while staff turnover disrupted safety knowledge. Indigenous practices and palm manuscripts faced intergenerational loss, yet digital systems introduced confidentiality, patient-anonymity, credential-verification and data-manipulation risks. Required capabilities include governed repositories, provenance, practitioner verification, privacy controls and succession arrangements. Without them, digitization relocates knowledge-loss risks into insecure or untrustworthy information systems (c211;c212;c214;c221;c227).
3. Clinical regulatory, ethical and institutional assurance (11). Biomedical research followed national ethics requirements; federated healthcare collaboration remained subject to privacy regulation; nuclear-facility training was restricted by confidentiality and radiation-exposure limits. Wearable blood-pressure models required compliance with IEEE and AAMI standards, while healthcare registries operated under statutory waiting-time rules. Deployment is therefore conditional on ethical approval, jurisdiction-specific privacy safeguards, safety standards and institutional eligibility controls (c210;c213;c214;c215;c227).
4. Lifecycle funding for medical models, devices and continuing education (5). Three-dimensional anatomical models incurred production costs, mixed-reality training required expensive headsets, and clinicians encountered financial barriers to in-person or lifelong education. Digital delivery may reduce travel and cadaver dependence, but it does not remove device acquisition, maintenance and programme-financing requirements. Sustained adoption therefore depends on lifecycle budgets and equitable equipment access (c209;c216;c218;c224).
Together, digitally reconfigured dependencies make clinical experience representable, computable and rehearsable; persistent dependencies provide expert judgement, governance, legitimacy and material continuity. Their interaction converts situated clinical and indigenous know-how into durable organizational knowledge, whereas weakness in either layer produces technically unreliable or institutionally unsupported retention.